Z||000000||newfie||04-26-2001||01:24 PM||||Just trying to get some general info before purchasing a Fiero.  <BR>1. I know the 88's are supposedly better but is it worth it considering they are much more expensive and parts are also more expensive, would it just be a better idea to get an older one and put the money into it.<BR>2. What are the major differences in the 4cyl and 6cyl especially in 88's? Are the trannys and drivetrain the same? Is it possible to just direct swap from 4 to 6.  Can the 4cyl running gear hold up to a bigger engine swap?<BR>3. How reliable is the 4banger, how many miles is it good for.  Any common problems. <BR>4. Will any parts from the pre88's fit the 88s or is the car a total redesign? <BR>5. Where is a good site to find out some general fiero facts as well as performance mods?<P>Anyway I'll probably have more Q's when I think of them.  I appreciate and info. <BR>thanks||||reg||5
Z||000001||KRMFiero||04-26-2001||01:43 PM||||I just rode in a 88 SE and it is ALOT better then my 84 and 85.. If you have the extra money i would try to get a 88, there is no way a pre88 could give that good of ride/handling, might be able to get close though with good shock,sprinks, coil overs<P>Well there is about a 40 hp difference from the 4 to the 6, the 6 cyl engine also looks cool and says Fiero on it.. I have only been in a 6cyl one before with a 4 speed and it was ALOT faster then my Duke's, I blive with the 88 they changed the crank or something i forgot, a 85 or newer fiero with a 4 cyl will take a 6 cyl with no mods - the 84 needs a different computer, relocate some stuff... The 4cyl running gear can hold up to a bigger engine, but be careful with the Automatic one - Its not that strong of a Tranny to begine with. I belive the strongest tranny avalable in the fiero was the 4 speed.<P>The 4 banger is well a Okay engine, its got soem speed with the 5 speed tranny, it will last a long time if it has been treated right, but chances  are whoever owned it before beat on it some times and it dont like that (btw: if you try to go faster then 5000 rpm with it, you'll be replaceing the engine)  The 4cyl in my 85 has 105K miles on and the engine works Great (Clutch or something just went though) my 84's with only 73K miles is gone, well spun a baring (i think)<P>Some parts of pre88's will fit on the 88, but the cradle and suspention is total different but can be adapted (very expensive) body panels, interier  can all be swapped (almost 100% sure)<P>If you get a duke i wouldnt try to do any performance mods because the engine already will probley have 100K+ miles on it and well i woulnt want to take the chance, maybe a new ignition   not sure about sites for thr 6 cyl.<P>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000002||Carrolles||04-26-2001||02:01 PM||||Take a look here for a wealth of Fiero info:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/biz/fierosails/info.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/biz/fierosails/info.html</A> <P>The 88 models have a different suspension on both ends.  Suspension parts (shocks, tie-rods, a-arms, brakes, etc) are not interchangeable between pre-88s and 88s.  The 88 suspension does not suffer bump steer like earlier models although you would not notice much difference unless you were pushing it pretty hard.<P>If you are looking to totally restore a Fiero and keep it for its future value then go for an 88 Formula or GT.  If you plan to simply enjoy the car any model will do.  <P>The 88 model suspension is better than the others but probably not enough for a daily driver to be worth the cost of upkeep.  Suspension parts for 88s are more expensive.  88s have vented rotors, earlier models have solid rotors.  All models have 4 wheel disk brakes.<P>Engines and transmissions are virtually the same.  Actually the 88 v-6 is listed as 135 hp while earlier v-6 are 140.  I believe the power is the same, just listed differently for political reasons.<P>87 and 88 models have a better designed headlight mechanism than earlier models.  They can be retrofitted into earlier models by swapping the motors and front end wiring harness.<P>Late 86 v-6 standard transmissions were Muncie/Getrag 5 speeds.  Earlier v-6 standard trans were Mincie 4 speeds.  The Getrag gets better mileage but costs much more to repair.  I believe the 4 cyl standard transmissions were Isuzu for all years (mostly).   I think all models used the same TH125 automatic transmissions regardless of engine.  88s (and I believe 87s) have a slightly larger fuel tank, 11+ gal vs. 10.3 gal.<P>The v-8 guys use all of the transmissions so I don't think there is an issue of one being suitable for either L4 or v-6.<P>Electrical systems and options are pretty much the same for all years (except 84 and 85 models had headrest speakers instead of rear sail panel speakers).<P>4 cyl to v-6 conversions are pretty involved but can be done.  If you get a 4 cyl car and want to upgrade in power you might as well look for something better than the stock Fiero 2.8L v-6.  The 93-95 Camaro/Fierbird 3.4L v-6 is a good upgrade and isn't too difficult to do.  The block is basically the same as the 2.8L engine but the starter has to be moved to the other side of the engine (must drill and tap a hole in an angled boss).  A couple of brackets are different and the Camaro/Firebird flywheel (or 88 Fiero one) must be used (there are others also that can be used).<P>Like I said, if you just want an enjoyable daily driver any year will do.<P>BTW,  welcome to the Forum.  Looking forward to seeing you at upcoming Fiero events.<P>I'm sure you'll get lots of discussion about these opinions but they are mine and fit my needs and wishes.||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||04-26-2001||02:48 PM||||Carolles and KRM, good synopsis.  What they just said.  The only thing I would point out  here is, probably one of the bigger headaches going from 4 cyl to V6 is that you will have to drop the fuel tank and change to the higher-pressure fuel pump to support the MFI the V6 uses.  <P>     ||||reg||14
